Ported code
-----------
crates/neighbors (milestone M9) is a Rust port of:

- Annoy 1.17.3, as bundled in RcppAnnoy 0.0.23 (inst/include/annoylib.h and
  kissrandom.h): Copyright (c) 2013 Spotify AB, licensed under the Apache
  License, Version 2.0 (third_party/licenses/Apache-2.0.txt). Changes: the
  index build (two_means, create_split, side, _make_tree, _split_imbalance),
  the search (_get_all_nns), the node layout and file format, and the
  Kiss64Random generator, for the Euclidean, Angular and Manhattan metrics,
  translated statement by statement into Rust (crates/neighbors/src/annoy.rs
  and kiss.rs); the multi-threaded build, on-disk build, mmap loading, the
  DotProduct and Hamming metrics and the other generators are not ported.
  Annoy's principal author is Erik Bernhardsson.
- RcppAnnoy 0.0.23 (Dirk Eddelbuettel, GPL (>= 2)): its conversions of R's
  doubles to the index's floats (addItem, getNNsByVectorList).
- Seurat 5.5.1 (Copyright 2021 Seurat authors, MIT): FindNeighbors' Annoy
  path (AnnoyBuildIndex, AnnoySearch, the k adjustment) and ComputeSNN
  (src/snn.cpp), in crates/neighbors/src/lib.rs and graph.rs.
- The GNU C++ library (libstdc++, GCC; GPL-3 with the GCC Runtime Library
  Exception): std::priority_queue's push_heap and pop_heap and
  std::partial_sort (bits/stl_heap.h, bits/stl_algo.h), whose exact moves
  Annoy's search depends on, in crates/neighbors/src/stl.rs.

crates/cluster (milestone M10) is a Rust port of:

- Seurat 5.5.1 (Copyright 2021 Seurat authors, MIT): src/ModularityOptimizer.cpp
  and src/RModularityOptimizer.cpp, Seurat's C++ translation of Ludo Waltman
  and Nees Jan van Eck's ModularityOptimizer 1.3.0 (the Java program of
  http://www.ludowaltman.nl/slm/, which implements their smart local moving
  algorithm and the Louvain algorithm of Blondel et al.). Changes: the
  JavaRandom generator (java.util.Random's nextInt, with its power-of-two
  branch and its 32-bit wrap-around) and generateRandomPermutation, the
  Clustering and Network classes (matrixToNetwork, createReducedNetwork,
  createSubnetworks), VOSClusteringTechnique's calcQualityFunction, local
  moving, Louvain, Louvain with multilevel refinement and smart local moving,
  and RunModularityClusteringCpp's random starts, best start, printed output
  and orderClustersByNNodes, translated statement by statement into Rust
  (crates/cluster/src/java_random.rs, clustering.rs, network.rs, vos.rs,
  optimizer.rs); the standalone main(), the edge-file reader,
  identifyComponents, removeCluster and removeSmallClusters, which
  FindClusters does not use, are not ported. The network and clustering are
  passed by reference instead of shared_ptr; the arithmetic and every order
  of operations are the C++'s.
- Seurat 5.5.1's R code (MIT): GroupSingletons, FindClusters.default's loop
  over the resolutions and FindClusters.Seurat's columns, level order and
  seurat_clusters (R/clustering.R), in crates/cluster/src/singletons.rs and
  find_clusters.rs. GroupSingletons' tie-breaking draw uses R's set.seed and
  sample, and its connectivity R's mean, from crates/rcompat (R, GPL (>= 2)).

crates/cluster (milestone M11) also ports:

- bluster 1.20.0 (Aaron Lun, GPL-3): approxSilhouette (R/approxSilhouette.R),
  translated into Rust in crates/cluster/src/silhouette.rs. Changes: the
  centroids, within-cluster variances and root-mean-square distances are
  computed with R's long-double colMeans, colSums and sum (from crates/rcompat)
  in the order R computes them; the closest other cluster is returned as a
  position among the levels present, and the widths of the workflow's guard
  (fewer than two cells or clusters) are NA, as add_silhouette_to_metadata
  leaves them.
- Seurat 5.5.1's and SeuratObject 5.4.0's R code (MIT): CreateSeuratObject's
  nCount_RNA and nFeature_RNA (.CalcN.default), in
  crates/cluster/src/metadata.rs; and the workflow's own
  require_min_cells_for_pca() (scRNAseq-preprocessing, silhouette_utils.R), in
  crates/cluster/src/chain.rs.

crates/knn and crates/cluster (milestone M12) port:

- bluster 1.20.0 (Aaron Lun, GPL-3): neighborPurity (R/neighborPurity.R) and
  its C++ sum_neighbor_weights (src/sum_neighbor_weights.cpp), translated into
  Rust in crates/cluster/src/purity.rs. Changes: each cell's per-cluster sums
  are taken from a table of repeated sums of the cluster's weight (every cell
  of a cluster has the same weight, so the sums equal the C++'s additions in
  any order); the totals are R's long-double rowSums (with crates/rcompat's
  long double) and the maxima R's max.col(ties.method = "first") (R 4.5.3's
  do_colsum and R_max_col, GPL (>= 2)); the maximum is returned
  as a position among the levels present; with fewer than two cells or
  clusters the purities are NA, as add_silhouette_to_metadata leaves them.
- BiocNeighbors 2.4.0 (Aaron Lun, GPL-3): findDistance's and findNeighbors'
  semantics as generic_find_knn and generic_find_all implement them (src/
  generics.cpp: k capped at the number of observations minus 1, the last
  distance only, 0 when no neighbour is left, the cell itself excluded),
  in crates/knn/src/purity.rs.
- knncolle (Copyright (c) 2023 Aaron Lun, MIT; the version vendored by
  assorthead 1.4.0, inst/include/knncolle/distances.hpp, NeighborQueue.hpp and
  report_all_neighbors.hpp; third_party/licenses/MIT-knncolle.txt):
  EuclideanDistance's raw, normalize and denormalize arithmetic, and the order
  in which neighbours are reported (by raw distance), in
  crates/knn/src/purity.rs. Changes: the search is exhaustive rather than
  knncolle_kmknn's KMKNN (Copyright (c) 2025 Aaron Lun, MIT), whose
  triangle-inequality bounds (knncolle_kmknn.hpp, as BiocNeighbors 2.4.0
  builds it) and whose k-means centres (kmeans, Copyright (c) 2021 Aaron Lun,
  MIT) are not ported but analysed, to bound where they could treat a pair
  differently (the certificate). Equal raw distances are reported by cell
  index, where KMKNN orders them by its own internal index.

crates/sctransform and crates/cluster (milestone M13) port:

- sctransform 0.4.3 (Christoph Hafemeister and Saket Choudhary, GPL-3):
  correct() (R/denoise.R), as vst() calls it with return_corrected_umi = TRUE
  (R/vst.R), and make.sparse() (R/utils.R): the regressors at the median of
  log_umi, the products per bin of genes, the variance at the median depth,
  the corrected values, round(x, 0), negatives to 0 and the zeros dropped,
  in crates/sctransform/src/correct.rs and residuals.rs. Changes: the
  corrected values are made in the same pass as vst's residuals (S2d), from
  each residual before vst clips it, one bin's products at a time, and kept
  sparse; the product at the median depth is kept as its runs of equal values
  per gene. R's round(x, 0) is nearbyint() (R 4.5.3's src/nmath/fround.c,
  GPL (>= 2)).
- Seurat 5.5.1 (MIT): SCTransform.Assay's SCT assay (R/preprocessing.R: the
  corrected counts as its counts layer, their log1p as its data layer) and
  the nCount_SCT and nFeature_SCT that adding the assay computes
  (SeuratObject 5.4.0's .CalcN), in crates/sctransform/src/correct.rs and
  crates/cluster/src/chain_output.rs.

The loaders, loaders/R/scrustio and loaders/python/scrustio (milestone M14),
adapt:

- Seurat 5.5.1 (MIT): PrepVSTResults() (R/objects.R), the SCTModel's feature
  and cell attributes as it assembles them from vst()'s result, in
  loaders/R/scrustio/R/sctmodel.R and loaders/python/scrustio/scrustio/
  sctmodel.py; SCTransform.Assay's and SCTransform.Seurat's assembly of the SCT
  assay (R/preprocessing.R: CreateAssayObject() on the corrected counts, the
  variable features, the data and scale.data layers, the SCTAssay class and its
  assay.orig), RunPCA.default's reduction (R/dimensional_reduction.R) and
  FindClusters.Seurat's identities (R/clustering.R), which
  loaders/R/scrustio/R/seurat.R makes with SeuratObject's and Seurat's own
  functions.
- SeuratObject 5.4.0 (MIT): CheckFeaturesNames()'s renaming of features
  (R/utils.R), with Read10X()'s make.unique(), in loaders/R/scrustio/R/names.R.
- sctransform 0.4.3 (Christoph Hafemeister and Saket Choudhary, GPL-3): the
  arguments vst() records (R/vst.R: its arguments with Seurat's values, and
  set_min_var) and its step-1 cells (sample(colnames(umi), n_cells) right
  after SCTransform's set.seed()), in sctmodel.R and sctmodel.py.
- The workflow's find_markers.R and combine_markers.R (scRNAseq-preprocessing,
  workflow/scripts/), copied with their source cited into
  validation/R/markers_from_loader.R, as M8's markers_clustering.R copies them.

The loaders fill the classes of SingleCellExperiment 1.32.0 (Davide Risso and
Aaron Lun, GPL-3: altExps, reducedDims, colPairs) and follow the conventions of
anndata 0.13.4 and scanpy 1.13.0 (BSD-3-Clause: uns["log1p"],
uns[<key>]["connectivities_key"], var_names_make_unique); no code of theirs is
copied.

scrust doubletfinder's re-clustering of the singlets (milestone M15,
crates/scrust/src/cmd_doubletfinder.rs) follows the workflow's doubletfinder.R
(scRNAseq-preprocessing, workflow/scripts/): after DoubletFinder it clusters the
singlets with the workflow's chain, and its PCA guard gives the message of
require_min_cells_for_pca(context = "doubletfinder") (silhouette_utils.R),
in crates/cluster/src/chain.rs.

The benchmark's harness, validation/benchmark/ (moved there from
data/benchmarks/scripts/ in milestone M15), runs copies of the workflow's
tenx2seuratrds.R, soupx.R, emptydrops.R, cellbender2seurat.R, doubletfinder.R
and silhouette_utils.R (scRNAseq-preprocessing, workflow/scripts/ at ea053fa),
each with phase-marker lines added and nothing else changed:
validation/benchmark/workflow/scripts/. make_workflow_copies.py checks that
removing the markers gives back each source byte for byte, and
validation/benchmark/workflow/SOURCES.tsv records each source's git blob and
SHA-256.

